{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OBSERVATION\n`.github/workflows/test.yml` triggers both `workflow_dispatch` and a 6-hour `schedule` on `main`, while `push` explicitly ignores `main`. Its workflow-level concurrency is `group: test-${{ github.ref }}` and `cancel-in-progress: true`. For both scheduled and manually-dispatched runs on `main`, `github.ref` is `refs/heads/main`, so both event types enter the identical group `test-refs/heads/main`. Read-only GitHub evidence confirms the ticket's incidents: run 33874909241 (`schedule`) was cancelled minutes after run 33875177644 (`workflow_dispatch`) started, and run 33905162633 (`workflow_dispatch`) was cancelled when run 33907581465 (`schedule`) started.\n\nHYPOTHESIS\nThe root cause is that the concurrency key omits the event/lane identity while cancellation is enabled. The prior design intentionally keyed by ref to bound redundant branch work, but on `main` the ref is constant and the schedule is the sole periodic main-health signal. Therefore manual health checks and scheduled health checks are treated as superseding instances of the same job, even though they are distinct readings whose loss should be observable.\n\nCONFIRMATION\nStatic evaluation of the workflow is deterministic: `schedule` on main -> `github.ref=refs/heads/main` -> group `test-refs/heads/main`; `workflow_dispatch` on main -> same `github.ref` -> same group; `cancel-in-progress: true` means whichever run starts second cancels the first. The GitHub run list above supplies real-world confirmation of both cancellation directions. This would have been disproven if the group included `github.event_name`/run id/sha for the main-health lane, if `cancel-in-progress` were false there, or if `main` also had an independent push health signal; none is true in current workflow.\n\nSUGGESTED FIX DIRECTION\nSeparate the main-health concurrency lane by event (or otherwise refuse/queue redundant dispatches with an explicit unavailable/superseded result) while preserving a documented bound on redundant full sweeps. Also make cancelled/superseded main-health runs report `UNAVAILABLE` distinctly from green/red so readers do not infer health from absence or cancellation."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788737331425304001,"uuid":"c7fe4338-f97e-40d9-adc2-9a27a3d56b85"}